For this project, keeping bun.lock is better right now, though pnpm is also a strong choice if you deploy outside AI Studio and convert your workspace config properly.
Here is how they compare for production:
1. Production Runtime: No Difference
Your app builds via vite build into static files (artifacts/pexo/dist/public in vercel.json). Neither Bun nor pnpm runs in production for end users—only the compiled browser bundle is served. The choice only affects CI/Vercel install and build reliability.
2. Why bun.lock Is Better for Your Current Setup
Already wired into package.json: Your root package.json uses "workspaces": ["artifacts/*"] and top-level "overrides" (which pin @reown/appkit* to 2.0.0 and patch transitive security vulnerabilities). Bun reads both natively.
Vercel & CI support: Vercel auto-detects bun.lock and runs bun install natively, and installs are typically faster than pnpm/npm.
AI Studio compatibility: This sandbox runs bun for compile_applet, lint_applet, and lockfile checks (bun install --frozen-lockfile passes cleanly).
3. When pnpm (pnpm-lock.yaml) Is Better
In general production engineering, pnpm has two advantages:
Stricter dependency isolation: By default, pnpm prevents code from importing transitive dependencies that aren't explicitly listed in package.json (avoiding "phantom dependency" bugs).
Wider ecosystem/CI maturity: Tools like GitHub Dependabot, legacy CI runners, and strict enterprise pipelines have longer-standing support for pnpm-lock.yaml (your .github/dependabot.yml currently has an empty package-ecosystem: "", and pnpm/npm are the most universally supported targets).
4. Important Warning If You Switch to pnpm
You cannot simply swap bun.lock for pnpm-lock.yaml. Because pnpm uses different config keys, switching without updating package.json would break your workspace and security patches:
pnpm ignores "workspaces" in package.json — you would need to create a pnpm-workspace.yaml file (packages: ["artifacts/*"]).
pnpm ignores top-level "overrides" in package.json — you would have to move "overrides" under "pnpm": { "overrides": { ... } } so your Reown and security patches still apply.
AI Studio's built-in checks use bun, so switching away from bun.lock is best done only if you are moving permanently to GitHub + Vercel.
Bottom Line
Stay on bun.lock while developing here and deploying to Vercel — it is fast, deterministic, and already matches your package.json workspaces and security overrides.
Switch to pnpm only after exporting permanently to GitHub if you want pnpm's strict node_modules isolation or Dependabot/CI tooling, and remember to add pnpm-workspace.yaml and "pnpm.overrides" when you do.
